artifact.self_contained_problem:
Consider primordial black holes (PBHs): hypothetical black holes formed in the early universe, with masses possibly extending well below a solar mass. A black hole of mass M emits Hawking radiation with characteristic temperature T_H = ℏc^3/(8π G M k_B), producing a thermal-like spectrum of Standard Model particles (photons, neutrinos, electrons/positrons, etc.) whose flux and spectral shape depend on M and on possible modifications of the emission rate (for example, near-extremal rotating or charged black holes, or higher-dimensional black holes, in which the effective Hawking emission is slowed or suppressed). For sufficiently small M, the evaporation accelerates and culminates in a final burst ("explosion") on a timescale shorter than current observation windows.

Determine whether next-generation gamma-ray and multimessenger observatories can detect, or place new constraints on, the following Hawking-evaporation signatures of a cosmological population of PBHs:

(a) the 511 keV gamma-ray line arising from annihilation of positrons produced by PBH evaporation in the interstellar/intergalactic medium;

(b) transient very-high-energy gamma-ray bursts from individual low-mass PBHs reaching the explosive late stage of evaporation;

(c) high-energy gamma-ray and neutrino fluxes from PBH populations whose Hawking emission is modified (slowed or suppressed), as occurs for near-extremal or higher-dimensional PBHs, thereby allowing the viable PBH mass window to extend to much lower masses than in the Schwarzschild four-dimensional case.

Specifically, identify the regions of PBH mass M and abundance f_PBH = Ω_PBH/Ω_DM (and, where relevant, spin or extra-dimensional parameters) for which each signature is detectable above astrophysical backgrounds, and the regions for which non-detection would yield improved upper limits on f_PBH.

